caption: Death and Fetters

When a person dies, they linger. For a number of hours after the moment of death equal to five  times their Will, they remain as a powerless wraith, a spirit in the dark umbra, able to see and hear the world but not interact with it (beyond the occasional Medium). After that time, they have a single opportunity, a moment of grace, where they can choose to accept the afterlife of whatever god or gods they worshipped in life. In many places, this offer is extended even to atheists and heathens, a final chance to accept the offer of divinity and go to whatever afterlife those beings offer. If they reject this, Daimon takes his turn. The Cosmic Force of Death pulls upon the soul, and like death this pull is inexorable rather than total. The new Wraith must roll their Will. A success means nothing happens: a moment of existential dread, then peace. Failure means their Will is reduced by one as oblivion takes a small piece of their resistance. A critical failure means they lose themselves and surrender to oblivion. This also happens if the Wraith is reduced to Will 2 or less, or they will themselves to let go, voluntarily failing the roll and surrendering to oblivion. A critical success means another offer of the afterlife, another chance at grace, but this is the last time for a long time.

Should the Wraith still exist after this first ordeal, they must distribute their remaining Will among Fetters; effectively, Gadget limitations on their Will, real objects with small representations they must keep on their person. A Fetter can be a person or an object, but it must have been important to the person in life. They can always be destroyed, and when they are, the part of the Wraith’s will allocated to that Fetter is gone forever. No Wraith can have more than Five fetters, and all new Will purchased must be connected to an existing Fetter.

Every year, on the anniversary of their death, as well as any time a Fetter is destroyed, the Wraith must roll Will again, like they did at death. However, critical success no longer offers a direct path to heaven, but rather an opportunity to work towards that goal. If the Wraith has sufficient free character points, they can buy off the Gadget limitation on one point of Will. These points of Will freed from their Fetters are the most vulnerable, and are lost first to failures on these will rolls. A Wraith freed from all Fetters who critically succeeds on a roll to resist oblivion is given another opportunity to go to their afterlife. If they refuse this call again, their will is re-bound to Fetters, this time reasons for them to refuse the call. This repeats until oblivion claims the soul or they finally retire to the afterlife. Of course, Wraiths don’t always know what afterlife awaits them, and other interventions can change this outcome (Resurrection, servitors that take the Wraith directly to the afterlife, etc).
